#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133480 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "abb40124-5eda-57b4-a815-9cd8b5661da1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5389" :question/problem "Plot the graph of the polar curve $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$." :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nwith the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/ad92e65ac8f604a06270a7bc13394a1c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133481 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line Given an Acute Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "6272bafb-8e57-583f-a320-f066fe40184b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5390" :question/problem "What is the polar equation of the ray shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/5bd17fb1994ce8f71ae772d3e8df931e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d90b9572-cdac-51f7-b386-81b750642585" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line That Passes Through a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "04f489ae-768f-5e8e-bed6-0a88f481c54c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5391" :question/problem "Given that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/24cabf2b7b7d2d79a92e8c18a568d670.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{11π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133483 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/id #uuid "e2c565be-0152-5088-8c4f-e66374cf2fb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5392" :question/problem "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P$, as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/1987662f932f5b8a82e413e10c2f9101.png)" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 7\\cos (\\frac{4π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= 7\\sin (\\frac{4π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "981ba4e7-33c9-5a7e-948f-f50fe4763a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132033} {:answer/id #uuid "b0585a0b-e1b5-5899-b51c-f2f6767c418d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3}, 4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132034} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9ee54c-fd00-5bee-aef8-d5893a35e049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3}, 4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132035} {:answer/id #uuid "41cfaaff-ed0d-596d-9958-df2dc78501d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3}, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132036} {:answer/id #uuid "60bff2cb-e1e3-5f42-9044-9ada3c2ae98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41cfaaff-ed0d-596d-9958-df2dc78501d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3}, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132036} :answer-field/id #uuid "738df0fd-bac8-51c6-8dab-997fca6c0b3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132032}] :question/id #uuid "d7c864f9-8a06-59dd-91e8-789300497ddc" :question/math-academy-id "q-41263" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2173/Source/Images/q-41263.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r = 8$ and the ray $θ = \\frac{5π}{6}$ intersect at the point $P$, as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?"}]}
